WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.868 --> 00:00:03.235
 Dustin and Weston,
congratulations.

00:00:03.335 --> 00:00:05.538
You fellas have made it to
the third and final round

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:07.107
of this competition.

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:09.008
Now, when you came here, we
had you make Japanese bushcraft

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:10.376
knives called natas.

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:12.045
Now we're sending you
back to your home forges

00:00:12.111 --> 00:00:14.680
to recreate this iconic
weapon from history.

00:00:14.747 --> 00:00:15.715
You ready to see what it is?

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:16.749
BOTH: Yes.

00:00:16.849 --> 00:00:17.550
 All right, that weapon is--

00:00:22.855 --> 00:00:25.090
the Zulu war axe.

00:00:25.157 --> 00:00:26.058
Good luck, bladesmiths.

00:00:26.158 --> 00:00:27.593
We'll see you in four days.

00:00:27.693 --> 00:00:28.327
 Good luck.

00:00:28.427 --> 00:00:29.561
 Good luck.

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:32.032 line:7%
[music playing]

00:00:33.166 --> 00:00:34.633
WESTON: Spent a
little bit of time

00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:37.037
in South Africa, so I've seen
some small versions of this.

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:40.239
I'm planning on doing a
Damascus exterior on both sides.

00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:44.076
And then I'm going to leave
a 80CRV2 internal core.

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:45.244
First weld on the
billet, it looks

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:46.345
like it's going really well.

00:00:46.446 --> 00:00:48.915
It's time to draw
the billet out.

00:00:49.015 --> 00:00:50.483 line:7%
I'll cut it and reforge it.

00:00:50.549 --> 00:00:54.187
That should give me
57 layers of Damascus.

00:00:54.254 --> 00:00:57.690
I'm going to twist that to
make a really cool pattern.

00:00:57.756 --> 00:01:01.294
Now it's time to stack it with
80CRV2 so that if something

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.296
happens with one of my
welds, the axe still

00:01:03.362 --> 00:01:05.231
has a good strong solid core.

00:01:05.297 --> 00:01:07.132
It was a good day of forging.

00:01:07.232 --> 00:01:09.469
I'll have to definitely
step it up tomorrow

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:10.303
to get back into it.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.004
 All right, that was fun.

00:01:12.071 --> 00:01:14.574 line:7%
[music playing]

00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:18.844
DUSTIN: So my goals for today
are to get the axe head roughly

00:01:18.911 --> 00:01:21.880
shaped, hopefully get the tang
drawn out to where I like it,

00:01:21.947 --> 00:01:24.016
and get bevels hammered in.

00:01:24.116 --> 00:01:26.185
I start by forging out
the top of the axe.

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:29.188
As I'm drilling it out,
I'm hammering bevels in.

00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:31.190
 Gotten about an
inch and a half.

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:32.758
2 and 1/2 more to go.

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:35.495
DUSTIN: I've got the Zulu axe
to where I would like it to be.

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:36.796
It's pretty well shaped.

00:01:36.863 --> 00:01:39.765
And now it's time to address
the oddly placed tang.

00:01:39.865 --> 00:01:42.568
Unfortunately, my opening is
just not big enough to allow

00:01:42.668 --> 00:01:43.836
me to go back and forth.

00:01:43.903 --> 00:01:48.507
So I've got to cut the whole
front of the forge out.

00:01:48.574 --> 00:01:51.777
And now, we have a nice big
opening to come straight in.

00:01:51.844 --> 00:01:55.414
The length of my tang is the
biggest obstacle right now.

00:01:55.515 --> 00:01:57.750
It's too skinny
as well as short.

00:01:57.816 --> 00:01:59.919
It's something I want to tackle
first thing in the morning,

00:01:59.986 --> 00:02:01.854
because if I can't
fix it, I want

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:03.890
to start over as
soon as possible

00:02:03.956 --> 00:02:06.058
rather than wasting any time.

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:07.927 line:7%
[music playing]

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:08.995 line:0%
WESTON: It's the
morning of day two.

00:02:09.095 --> 00:02:10.596
I'm working on the billet.

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:13.699
Game plan is to split it and
start forging out the tang.

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:15.601
I've noticed that
there's a couple cracks.

00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:18.037
I'm going to grind down
through those areas

00:02:18.104 --> 00:02:22.008
and see if they grind out as I
get closer to my final shape.

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:24.343
My plan for getting
the tang attached,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:27.680
I'm going to cut a little
notch to the length of my tang.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.384
 So we'll heat it up, chisel
down, push that out, and draw

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.253
out our tang, finish our blade.

00:02:33.353 --> 00:02:35.588
WESTON: Now it's just drawing
it out to length to make sure

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:36.789
I meet parameters.

00:02:36.889 --> 00:02:39.592
Going into the clinch, I'm
feeling a little nervous.

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:42.327
Those cracks in the blade
that I'd seen earlier,

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:43.897
I think I've got
them ground down.

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:50.837
I think we dodged a
bullet on this one.

00:02:50.904 --> 00:02:53.305 line:0%
I think it's
looking really good.

00:02:53.372 --> 00:02:55.441 line:0%
DUSTIN: So I start today off
with a problem with my tang.

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:59.445
My tang is just way too
skinny as well as short.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:01.480
I cut two pieces of
high carbon steel,

00:03:01.547 --> 00:03:03.115
fit them up next to my tang.

00:03:03.215 --> 00:03:06.518
I weld them in to make
it wider and longer.

00:03:06.585 --> 00:03:08.888
But with so much going
right, eventually

00:03:08.954 --> 00:03:10.557
something's going to go wrong.

00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:11.957
 My breaker blew.

00:03:12.024 --> 00:03:13.859
I'll be back in one second.

00:03:13.926 --> 00:03:16.329
DUSTIN: I really hope this
isn't a reoccurring issue today.

00:03:16.396 --> 00:03:18.497
I have a lot of work to do
when I build my quench tank,

00:03:18.564 --> 00:03:22.835
and there's a lot of
welding involved to do so.

00:03:22.936 --> 00:03:25.504
My quench tank is going
to be like a trough style.

00:03:25.604 --> 00:03:27.440
I'm going to
horizontally quench it.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.309
So I'm getting ready to
weld, and I'm thinking,

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:31.810
it's going to be fine.

00:03:31.877 --> 00:03:34.880
As soon as it tries to start,
poof, my breaker goes out.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.248
 [bleep]

00:03:36.315 --> 00:03:38.450
DUSTIN: So back and
forth and back and forth.

00:03:38.517 --> 00:03:40.019
So, many, many
trips to the breaker

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:42.221
later, I have a
finished quench tank.

00:03:42.287 --> 00:03:43.122
 I feel good now.

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:44.457
I'm happy.

00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:46.392
DUSTIN: What a pain in
the ass that was, man.

00:03:46.458 --> 00:03:50.162
Now I've got to get it quenched.

00:03:50.229 --> 00:03:52.397
So I go into the
oil, and I've got

00:03:52.497 --> 00:03:54.634
this thing quenched straight.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:56.836
It skates like glass,
solid as an oak.

00:03:56.936 --> 00:04:00.072 line:7%
I couldn't be happier.

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:01.573 line:7%
WESTON: Top of day four.

00:04:01.673 --> 00:04:04.309 line:0%
My game plan for today is put
the final edge on the blade

00:04:04.409 --> 00:04:07.113
and then try to get the
wood section of this

00:04:07.179 --> 00:04:08.447
build figured out.

00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:09.916
I've got a section
of Osage orange

00:04:10.016 --> 00:04:11.850
that I've been working on,
and I've got another section

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:13.018
of African ebony.

00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:14.587
I'd really like
to use the ebony.

00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:17.456
I think the black would work
great with the Damascus.

00:04:17.523 --> 00:04:19.025
The best way to
tell if this handle

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:20.493
will hold up to
the judges' testing

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.795
is to test it here first.

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:25.431
[record scratch]

00:04:25.498 --> 00:04:26.465
 Well.

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:27.833
WESTON: Just what you
don't want to have

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:30.269
happen to your expensive
beautiful piece of ebony.

00:04:30.369 --> 00:04:32.805
But better here than there.

00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:35.374
Now it's back to the
Osage orange handle.

00:04:35.474 --> 00:04:38.243
Before I do the final glue-up
and get my blade mounted

00:04:38.344 --> 00:04:40.879
to the axe handle, I want
to get the final edge

00:04:40.979 --> 00:04:42.548
put on the blade.

00:04:42.648 --> 00:04:45.451
The convex grind I put on this
should have enough backing

00:04:45.518 --> 00:04:47.820
behind it to keep the edge
from having any damage

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.355
if it hits wood or bone.

00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:50.889
 It's looking sharp.

00:04:50.989 --> 00:04:53.359
WESTON: Time's up, and there's
nothing else I can do to it.

00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:54.626
I feel like it's
going to perform well.

00:04:54.726 --> 00:04:56.695
It should be able to
handle all the tests.

00:04:56.762 --> 00:04:57.296
 Liking it.

00:04:57.396 --> 00:04:59.098 line:7%
It's looking good.

00:04:59.165 --> 00:05:01.934 line:0%
DUSTIN: The plans for today
are to do some more grinding

00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:04.369
on my axe head, and I've
got to do a little bit

00:05:04.436 --> 00:05:05.938
more work to my handle.

00:05:06.038 --> 00:05:08.374
I'm going to spend a little time
on the grinder cleaning it up,

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:11.611
getting it to where
I want it to be.

00:05:11.678 --> 00:05:14.981
Now the blade is 90% done
without an edge on it.

00:05:15.048 --> 00:05:16.983
It's time to get
back to my handle.

00:05:17.083 --> 00:05:19.719
 So now I'm going to do some
burn work on this handle.

00:05:19.785 --> 00:05:21.420
I've never done it
before, so I hope

00:05:21.486 --> 00:05:23.322
it really makes the grain pop.

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:25.591
It definitely came
out really nice.

00:05:25.657 --> 00:05:27.226
DUSTIN: So now to
finish the blade,

00:05:27.292 --> 00:05:28.594
I'm going to do an acid edge.

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:33.399
 I'm really stoked right now.

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:35.901
It's just better than
I could have imagined.

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:37.536
DUSTIN: So I put a convex
edge on it, something

00:05:37.636 --> 00:05:39.972
that can withstand
smashing, but can

00:05:40.072 --> 00:05:42.975
also be sharp enough to slice.

00:05:43.042 --> 00:05:44.477
 Awesome.

00:05:44.544 --> 00:05:47.480
 Your Zulu war axes
look quite scary.

00:05:47.546 --> 00:05:49.247
But it's time to find out
what kind of lethal damage

00:05:49.314 --> 00:05:50.516
they can do.

00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:51.984
To find it out, I
will take your weapon,

00:05:52.084 --> 00:05:55.420
deliver some chops and
slashes on this boar carcass.

00:05:55.487 --> 00:05:56.222
Dustin, you're up first.

00:05:56.322 --> 00:05:57.022
You ready for this?

00:05:57.123 --> 00:05:57.790
 Let's do it.

00:05:57.857 --> 00:06:00.360
[music playing]

00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:18.778
 Knew it.

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:27.653
 Well done, sir.

00:06:27.754 --> 00:06:28.987
 Thank you, sir.

00:06:29.054 --> 00:06:31.457
 All right, Dustin, let's
talk about your Zulu war axe.

00:06:31.523 --> 00:06:32.792
It's quite surprising
that something

00:06:32.859 --> 00:06:35.327
this light with
not a lot of metal

00:06:35.427 --> 00:06:38.464
can cut deep into
this boar carcass.

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:40.466
Your blade stayed true.

00:06:40.532 --> 00:06:42.168
The handle construction
is ovoid enough

00:06:42.268 --> 00:06:46.071
to where even though it tapers,
I have a very good feel for it.

00:06:46.138 --> 00:06:49.708
Overall, sir, it will kill.

00:06:49.808 --> 00:06:50.876
 Thank you.

00:06:50.976 --> 00:06:52.044
 All right, Weston, your turn.
So you ready?

00:06:52.111 --> 00:06:52.811
 Yes, sir.

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:55.181
[music playing]

00:07:10.396 --> 00:07:13.299
 All right, Weston, let's talk
about your Zulu war axe here.

00:07:13.366 --> 00:07:16.902
What I find interesting here is
that the design of this blade,

00:07:16.969 --> 00:07:19.504
there's a sweet spot in here
with a little bit of weight.

00:07:19.604 --> 00:07:22.608
So that first swing
alone cut in very deep.

00:07:22.708 --> 00:07:25.578
There are no glints
or rolls on it.

00:07:25.644 --> 00:07:27.246
The handle construction
is ovoid enough

00:07:27.346 --> 00:07:30.482
to where I get a good grip and
I can get a good feel on it.

00:07:30.582 --> 00:07:33.385
And more importantly,
sir, it will kill.

00:07:33.452 --> 00:07:34.120
 Thank you.

00:07:36.789 --> 00:07:39.358
 Bladesmiths, welcome
to the strength test--

00:07:39.424 --> 00:07:40.960
the ammo box chop.

00:07:41.026 --> 00:07:43.762
I'm going to test the strength
and durability of your axes

00:07:43.862 --> 00:07:45.932
by smashing them into
these ammo boxes.

00:07:46.032 --> 00:07:47.666
Remember, this test
is all about what

00:07:47.766 --> 00:07:50.235
those boxes do to your axes
and not the other way around.

00:07:50.336 --> 00:07:51.737
Dustin, you're up first.
You ready?

00:07:51.838 --> 00:07:52.270
 Let's do it.

00:07:52.371 --> 00:07:54.607
[music playing]

00:08:03.382 --> 00:08:04.717
[metal clangs]

00:08:04.783 --> 00:08:09.255
[sighs] [clicks tongue] Damn it.

00:08:12.191 --> 00:08:15.527
 Well, Dustin, we've got
an obvious issue here.

00:08:15.627 --> 00:08:17.563
Now, I'm looking at
your grain in here.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:18.530
It's beautiful.

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:20.832
It's nice and velvety tight.

00:08:20.932 --> 00:08:26.772
But right here, there's a little
dark spot which was a crack.

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:30.776
It happened right where the join
of your tang and your blade is.

00:08:30.843 --> 00:08:33.245
And that little crack
is a stress riser,

00:08:33.345 --> 00:08:34.413
and it leads to big failure.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:35.147
 Sure.

00:08:41.654 --> 00:08:42.955
 All right, Dustin,
your blade has

00:08:43.022 --> 00:08:45.724
broke on the third strike
against these ammo boxes

00:08:45.791 --> 00:08:47.293
in our strength test.

00:08:47.359 --> 00:08:49.262
Now, that doesn't mean that
you're out of this competition.

00:08:49.328 --> 00:08:52.264
Your competitor still has
to endure the same rigors.

00:08:52.331 --> 00:08:53.733
Weston, that means
that your blade

00:08:53.799 --> 00:08:57.769
must survive three strikes
against these ammo boxes.

00:08:57.870 --> 00:08:59.871
And if it does, you'll be
the new "Forged in Fire"

00:08:59.971 --> 00:09:02.474
champion taking home
that check for 10 grand.

00:09:02.541 --> 00:09:03.509
You guys ready for this?

00:09:03.609 --> 00:09:04.844
- Let's do it.
- Let's do it.

00:09:04.911 --> 00:09:05.611
 All right, Ben.

00:09:08.547 --> 00:09:10.182
 All right, Weston,
how are you feeling?

00:09:10.249 --> 00:09:10.949
 Nervous.

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:13.553
[music playing]

00:09:24.396 --> 00:09:25.097
 Good job.

00:09:25.197 --> 00:09:25.831
 Thanks.

00:09:29.401 --> 00:09:30.536
 Well, Weston, it held up.

00:09:30.636 --> 00:09:31.537
Nice job.

00:09:31.604 --> 00:09:33.439
This is a nice light axe.

00:09:33.539 --> 00:09:35.074
A lot of fun to swing.

00:09:35.141 --> 00:09:37.977
With every swing, I could feel
the reverberation, just it kind

00:09:38.077 --> 00:09:39.645
of jiggling in my hands.

00:09:39.712 --> 00:09:40.679
It was singing.

00:09:40.779 --> 00:09:41.413
Really well done.

00:09:41.513 --> 00:09:42.214
 Thank you.

00:09:42.281 --> 00:09:43.182
Thank you.

00:09:43.282 --> 00:09:44.516
 Weston, congratulations.

00:09:44.583 --> 00:09:46.051
Your blade held up.

00:09:46.118 --> 00:09:49.521
Dustin, that means that your
blade doesn't make the cut.

00:09:49.621 --> 00:09:51.089
I'd like to invite you
to shake our hands,

00:09:51.189 --> 00:09:55.260
shake your competitor's hand,
and then exit the forge.

00:09:55.361 --> 00:09:56.095
Great job, man.

00:09:56.162 --> 00:09:57.830
 Thank you, sir.

00:09:57.896 --> 00:09:59.131
DUSTIN: I'm pretty bummed out.

00:09:59.198 --> 00:10:01.300
I really wanted to
win this whole thing.

00:10:01.366 --> 00:10:02.367
 Weston, congratulations, bud.

00:10:02.467 --> 00:10:03.169
 Thank you.

00:10:03.269 --> 00:10:04.503
Good job.

00:10:04.569 --> 00:10:05.337
DUSTIN: But this has
been one of the greatest

00:10:05.437 --> 00:10:06.906
experiences of my life.

00:10:06.972 --> 00:10:08.507
This is not the result I wanted.

00:10:08.574 --> 00:10:12.177
But at the end of the day, I'm
here, I'm alive, I'm healthy,

00:10:12.244 --> 00:10:13.212
so I'm happy.

00:10:16.182 --> 00:10:17.283
 Weston, congratulations.

00:10:17.383 --> 00:10:18.650
You made a beautiful
Zulu war axe,

00:10:18.751 --> 00:10:21.020
and that makes you the
"Forged in Fire" champion.

00:10:21.086 --> 00:10:24.089
That's a title that comes
with a check for $10,000.

00:10:24.189 --> 00:10:25.690
Come forward and shake
our hands, my friend.

00:10:25.757 --> 00:10:27.359
WESTON: I am the "Forged
in Fire" champion,

00:10:27.459 --> 00:10:28.526
and I feel elated.

00:10:28.627 --> 00:10:29.928
I came here to
prove that I could

00:10:29.995 --> 00:10:32.231
compete with some
incredibly talented smiths,

00:10:32.331 --> 00:10:33.465
and I came out on top.

00:10:33.565 --> 00:10:34.432
It's been a blast.

00:10:34.533 --> 00:10:37.236
[music playing]

